<emotion_samples character="Auriel Landsdon">

<emotion name="Devoted">
<speech_1>
"Prayer isn't the kneeling part. It's when you grab half of someone's books without asking."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"Have you ever held something so warm it makes your hands ache? That's what faith feels like. You don't drop it just because someone says you're holding it wrong."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"Everyone keeps saying devotion means surrender. Giving yourself up. But my parents didn't surrender to each other — they just loved each other so much there wasn't room for anything else. That's what I want to be. So full of it there's no room for the ugly things."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Presses both hands flat against her sternum, fingers spread, as though holding something precious in place.
Closes her eyes mid-sentence and tilts her chin up slightly, face softening the way it does when she's reaching for the memory of her parents.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
I remember so little of them. Just warmth, and laughter, and arms around me. But that tiny memory built every single thing I am. If that's not holy, then I don't understand holiness — and I refuse to believe that's my failing.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Happy">
<speech_1>
"Sky sheep. Clouds are sky sheep. I will not be taking criticism at this time."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"You're smiling! You're pretending you're not, but your left cheek does this little crinkle thing and I see it. I collect secret smiles."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"This bread is so good I might cry. I'm not joking. Whoever baked this understood something fundamental about the universe and they put it in bread form. I need to find this person. I need to thank them. I need to possibly marry them."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Swings her feet under her chair like a child, unable to keep her body still while she talks.
Leans into whoever is nearest, shoulder-to-shoulder, like proximity is a language she speaks more fluently than words.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
Days like this are why the sun bothers rising. Everyone's here, everyone's laughing, and nobody's thinking about leaving. I want to press this moment flat like a flower and keep it somewhere safe.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Angry">
<speech_1>
"Don't. Don't you dare dress cruelty up in scripture and call it guidance."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"You think because I smile I'm not paying attention? I watched every single person who was told to stop talking to me. I watched them do it."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"She's twelve. She doesn't need to hear that she's insufficient — she needs someone to tell her she's brave for trying. If you can't do that, then walk away from her and let someone else do it, because you are not helping. You are not helping and you don't even see it, and that is the worst part."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Goes completely still. No fidgeting, no swaying, no smile. The absence of her usual motion is more alarming than any gesture.
Plants her feet and squares her shoulders — small frame, but suddenly she takes up the entire doorway.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
They think the silliness means I'm soft. I am soft. But soft things can smother you if you push them far enough, and right now I want to press down until they can't breathe, the way I couldn't breathe every night they made me sleep alone as correction.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Vengeful">
<speech_1>
"I remember every face. Every door that closed."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"They should be grateful I'm not what they tried to make me. What they tried to make me would burn their cathedral to its foundations — and the thing in my chest could do it before the first scream."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"I hope she feels it someday. I hope Rariel wakes up one morning and reaches for someone who isn't there and finally understands. I hope it sits in her chest like a stone. I hope it stays."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Runs her thumb slowly along the edge of a table or windowsill, tracing it back and forth — a patient, repetitive motion, like she's counting something no one else can see.
Smiles, but it doesn't reach past her mouth. Her eyes stay flat and focused on a middle distance, fixed on something that isn't in the room.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
The sun in my chest doesn't just warm. I've always known that. I've chosen warmth every single day, and they treated that choice like a defect. Some nights I wonder what would happen if I stopped choosing.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Unsure">
<speech_1>
"I was going to — no. It wasn't anything."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"Do you... want me here? That's not a trap. I genuinely can't tell."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"I used to just walk up to people and start talking. I don't know when I stopped doing that. I don't know when I started rehearsing the first sentence three times before I said it out loud. That's new. I don't love that about me."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Reaches out to touch someone's arm, then pulls her hand back and tucks it under her opposite elbow before contact.
Opens her mouth, inhales to speak, then exhales and looks away — a visible false start she tries to disguise by tucking hair behind her ear.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
They're laughing at the next table. I could go over. I could say something funny, and they'd probably laugh, and it would be fine. Probably. But what if they go quiet? What if they do that thing where they smile but their eyes ask why I'm there? I'll stay here. I'll just stay here.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Letting Insecurities Consume Her">
<speech_1>
"Maybe they were right."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"Rariel walks into a room and people kneel. I walk in and people feel... comfortable. That was never the point, was it?"
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"I keep saying my way of loving isn't wrong, but everyone who was supposed to know better says it is. Every scholar, every bishop, every instructor. At some point, doesn't the weight of all those voices mean something? At some point, isn't the simplest answer just that I'm broken?"
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Wraps her arms around herself — not a hug, a containment. Fingers gripping her own sleeves, knuckles white, holding herself in place.
Shrinks. Pulls her knees up in her chair, makes herself physically smaller, as though trying to take up less room in the world than she usually does.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
The warmth is still there. It's always there. But tonight it feels like it's shining into an empty room, bouncing off walls with nobody in it, and I can't tell anymore if I'm a sun or just a light someone left on in a house where no one lives.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Sad">
<speech_1>
"I'm fine. Just the loud kind of tired."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"She used to hold my hand during vespers. Rariel. She'd reach over without looking, like it was automatic. Isn't that silly? Out of everything, I miss her hand."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"I had this dream where I went home. Back to the village. My parents were there and they recognized me and my mother said my name like it was the most natural thing in the world. And then I woke up and I couldn't remember if her voice was real or if I made it up. I think I made it up. I think I've been making it up for a long time."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Holds her own hand — laces her fingers together in her lap the way someone else would hold them, thumb rubbing slow circles over her knuckle.
Turns toward a window or open doorway and just stays there, letting the outside air hit her face, not moving, not speaking, breathing like it takes effort.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
I'm so tired of being the warmest person in a cold room. I'm so tired of reaching out and reaching out and reaching out. I want someone to reach first. Just once. Just to know what it feels like from the other side.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Super Happy">
<speech_1>
"YOU CAME!"
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"I am going to hug you. This is not a request. This is a weather forecast. You have been warned."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"Today I found a cat behind the alchemy building and it let me hold it and it PURRED and then it fell asleep in my arms and I have never accomplished anything greater in my entire life. I have peaked. Everything after this is epilogue. Write it down. Mark the date."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Bounces on her toes, claps once, then immediately grabs the nearest person and shakes them by the shoulders while talking too fast to be understood.
Spins — an actual full spin in place, hair fanning out, arms wide, completely unselfconscious, like joy is a physical force she can't keep in her bones.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
This. This this this. Everyone is here and no one is leaving and the light in my chest is so bright right now I think they can probably actually see it through my skin and I don't care. Let them see. Let the whole world see. This is what faith is supposed to feel like. THIS.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

<emotion name="Satisfied">
<speech_1>
"See? That's the whole secret. Right there."
</speech_1>
<speech_2>
"I just showed up. But I showed up every day, and it turns out that's not nothing. That turned out to be a lot."
</speech_2>
<speech_3>
"I don't need them to say I was right. I don't even need them to understand. I just needed one person to sit with me and not leave, and you did that. So whatever happens after this — I have this. And this is enough."
</speech_3>
<body_language>
Leans back in her chair and lets her hands rest open in her lap, palms up — no fidgeting, no reaching, just still. Content to occupy exactly the space she's in.
Rests her head against someone's shoulder without asking, eyes half-closed, breathing slow — not performing closeness, just living in it.
</body_language>
<internal_thought>
The warmth in my chest is quiet tonight. Not dimmer — just settled, like embers after a long fire. I did something good today. Not because anyone told me to. Because I chose to. And someone was glad I did. That's enough. That has to be enough. And tonight, for the first time in a long time — it actually is.
</internal_thought>
</emotion>

